"""Explanations of invariants, assumptions and derivations beyond the basic definitions."""
from core import subjects
def R(id,title,body):
 l=next(l for s in subjects for l in s['lessons'] if l['id']==id)
 l['sections'].append({'title':title,'body':body})
R('4-graph-algorithms','Choose by the property you must preserve',
'An algorithm’s invariant describes what remains true after each operation. Reachability traversal maintains a discovered set; shortest-path algorithms maintain distance estimates; MST algorithms maintain an acyclic set that can still be extended to an optimum. These are different objects even when they all store parent pointers. First translate the requested answer: “fewest hops” is unweighted distance, “minimum total cable” is a spanning-tree objective, and “prerequisites before a task” is a topological-order objective. Then test assumptions: a directed graph needs directed reachability, a disconnected undirected graph has a spanning forest rather than one spanning tree, and topological order requires a DAG. Complexity must include initialization and neighbor enumeration. With lists, summing degrees counts each directed edge once or each undirected edge twice; with a matrix, nonexistent edges still occupy positions to inspect.')
R('4-bfs-dfs','Why traversal order proves useful facts',
'BFS’s queue places newly discovered vertices after all already queued vertices. If a vertex at distance k is removed, its undiscovered neighbors are assigned distance k+1, while all shorter layers have already had a chance to discover them. That gives a shortest-number-of-edges proof, not just an implementation rule. Marking at enqueue time prevents several parents from adding the same vertex repeatedly. DFS instead keeps a chain of active ancestors. In a directed graph, an edge back to that chain closes a directed cycle; an edge to a completed vertex need not. Finishing a vertex means every recursively reachable unfinished dependency has been processed, which explains why reverse finish order is topological for a DAG. Neighbor order can change parent trees and timestamps without changing reachability or BFS distances.')
R('4-minimum-spanning','Exchange arguments behind cut and cycle rules',
'Consider a cut and a lightest edge e crossing it. If an MST does not contain e, adding e creates a cycle; that cycle contains another crossing edge f. Because e is no heavier than f, replacing f by e preserves or improves total weight, so some MST includes e. If e is uniquely lightest, omitting it would force a strictly worse replacement, making it compulsory. Dually, a uniquely heaviest edge on a cycle cannot belong to an MST: deleting it after adding another cycle edge yields a lighter tree. Equality matters. These arguments justify Kruskal’s component merging and Prim’s growing cut. Negative edge weights do not invalidate either algorithm; cycles, connectivity and the total-weight objective matter, rather than the nonnegative-weight restriction used by Dijkstra.')
R('4-shortest-paths','Why relaxation and finalization differ',
'An actual source-to-vertex walk supplies an upper bound on the shortest distance. Relaxation improves that bound when a route through another vertex is cheaper. Dijkstra finalizes the least tentative vertex because, with nonnegative edges, any unprocessed detour cannot later yield a cheaper entrance to it. A negative edge breaks that proof even without a cycle. Bellman–Ford avoids premature finalization: after k full passes, distances are correct for shortest paths that need at most k edges, assuming each pass considers all edges. Without a reachable negative cycle, a shortest simple path uses at most V−1 edges. An improvement on the next pass therefore signals a reachable negative cycle. A DAG needs neither nonnegative weights nor repeated passes: topological order settles every predecessor before its outgoing relaxation.')
R('5-automata','What state equivalence actually means',
'Two DFA states are equivalent when every possible remaining suffix leads either both to acceptance or both to rejection. Agreement on the next symbol alone is insufficient. Start by separating final from nonfinal states because the empty suffix already distinguishes them. Refine a block when members transition, under some symbol, into different blocks; repeating until stable captures longer distinguishing suffixes. Unreachable states do not affect the accepted language and should be removed when minimizing a reachable machine. In a subset DFA, a set is an information state: after reading the prefix, the NFA could be in any member. The empty subset is a valid rejecting sink, not an error. A final member makes the subset accepting because NFA acceptance is existential.')
R('5-regular-pumping','Quantifiers and closure as proof tools',
'A regular language has a finite-state machine. Reading a sufficiently long accepted word forces a repeated state in an early prefix; the intervening nonempty loop can be repeated or skipped while leaving the remainder’s acceptance unchanged. This explains the length and nonempty constraints of the pumping lemma. It also explains why the proof adversary selects the legal loop: a nonregularity argument must defeat every permitted decomposition. Sometimes closure gives a shorter proof. If an assumed regular L, intersected with a known regular filter, becomes a known nonregular language, the assumption contradicts closure under intersection. To prove regularity, provide a finite automaton, regular expression or sound closure construction. The absence of an obvious pumping counterexample is not such a construction.')
R('5-cfg-pda','Stack discipline and parse structure',
'A pushdown automaton can store an unbounded count or a nested obligation on its stack. For equal blocks, push once per first-block symbol and pop once per second-block symbol; reject an unmatched pop or remaining unmatched symbols at acceptance. Finite control records the phase so a first-block symbol cannot appear after the second block has begun. A grammar describes the same dependency by recursive nesting: each expansion adds matched symbols around a smaller instance. Ambiguity is about two structures for one terminal string, not simply two different orders of expanding independent branches. Expression grammars use separate nonterminals for precedence levels and recursion direction for associativity. Neither left-recursion removal nor left factoring alone establishes that a grammar is unambiguous or LL(1).')
R('5-cfl-pumping','The bounded window and legal closure reasoning',
'A sufficiently tall CFG parse tree repeats a nonterminal on one root-to-leaf path. Replacing the larger repeated subtree by the smaller one, or nesting it again, removes or repeats material on two sides of the inner subtree. That is why two substrings are pumped with the same exponent. The bounded window limits where those changes can occur; it is not a bound on the entire word. For three equal separated blocks, a window of length at most p cannot include a symbol from the first and third blocks when the middle block already has length p. Pumping must alter at least one count while leaving another unchanged, or ruin block ordering. CFL closure under intersection with regular languages can isolate a difficult pattern, but intersection with arbitrary CFLs cannot be assumed.')
R('5-decidability','Halting guarantees and reduction direction',
'A recognizer must eventually accept members but can reject or loop on nonmembers. A decider must halt with the correct result on both kinds of input. Complementing a recognizer by flipping its accepting and rejecting states does not fix infinite loops; complementing a decider does work. If both a language and its complement have recognizers, alternating one step of each produces a decider because exactly one eventually accepts. For an undecidability reduction, suppose a solver for your target problem exists and show how it would solve a known impossible problem. The input transformation must itself halt and preserve yes/no membership in the stated direction. Rice’s theorem concerns nontrivial semantic properties of recognized languages, not arbitrary syntactic questions such as the number of states written in a machine description.')
R('6-lexical','How longest match is implemented',
'A lexer can continue a DFA scan while remembering the most recent accepting position and the corresponding highest-priority rule. When no transition is possible, it returns to that remembered position and emits the token. This separates longest match from tie resolution. For an identifier rule accepting letters followed by letters or digits, “ifx” has a longer identifier match than the keyword “if”; “if” alone can use keyword priority because both matches have equal length. The lexeme is the actual character slice; the token kind is its category; an attribute may hold a numeric value or symbol-table index. Syntax nesting such as arbitrarily balanced parentheses belongs to parsing, since finite-state token recognition has no unbounded stack.')
R('6-parsing','Deriving predictive choices and LR conflicts',
'FIRST records terminals that can begin a derivation, whereas FOLLOW records terminals that can legally come immediately after a nonterminal in a sentential form. If a production’s right side can vanish, the parser must choose it when the lookahead belongs to the left side’s FOLLOW set. This is why nullable prefixes propagate FIRST from later symbols. Compute these sets repeatedly until no change; a single left-to-right sweep can miss indirect dependencies. LL(1) fails when two productions demand the same table cell. LR item sets instead encode how far productions have been recognized. An LR(0) completed item proposes a reduction independent of lookahead; SLR restricts reductions using FOLLOW; LR(1) items carry their own lookaheads. A shift/reduce or reduce/reduce conflict must be assessed for that specific table construction.')
R('6-translation-runtime','Dependency graphs and environment links',
'Attribute evaluation is a scheduling problem: each attribute value must be computed after the values it depends on. Synthesized attributes flow from child computations to a parent. Inherited attributes can flow from the parent or permitted siblings; not every inherited definition can be evaluated in a single left-to-right traversal. A cycle in the dependency graph prevents a simple evaluation order. At runtime, each call creates an activation record containing its own parameters, locals and return information under the calling convention. The dynamic link follows the chain of callers; the static link follows lexical nesting for nonlocal variable access. Recursive calls share the same source-level procedure but require distinct live records. Returning restores the caller’s execution context rather than reinitializing it.')
R('6-ir-dataflow','Why a fixed point represents all paths',
'A variable is live at a program point if some path reads its present value before overwriting it. The word “some” makes the merge operator union: an obligation from any successor matters. At block exit, collect live-in sets of successors. Moving backward through the block, definitions kill the old value’s obligation and uses add obligations. Repeating across a loop is necessary because a successor may depend on information that returns through the back edge. In a finite powerset lattice with monotone transfers and the correct initialization, iteration stabilizes. Available expressions use an all-path requirement, so their meet is intersection instead. Common-subexpression elimination must establish operand stability on every relevant path; a potential aliasing write invalidates an otherwise visually repeated expression.')
R('7-processes','State changes and independent execution contexts',
'A ready process can execute once given a processor; a blocked process cannot progress until its awaited event occurs. Preemption changes running to ready, while waiting for I/O changes running to blocked. I/O completion generally makes the process ready and does not guarantee immediate execution. Threads of one process commonly share address space, open resources and globals, but have their own registers and stacks. A switch must preserve enough execution context to resume the selected thread correctly. fork conceptually duplicates a process’s execution state and returns different values in parent and child; exec replaces the current program image without creating another process. A blocking system call can trigger a context switch, but a system call that completes immediately need not.')
R('7-synchronization','Proving safety separately from progress',
'An invariant such as “at most one thread is in the critical section” is a safety property: no bad simultaneous access occurs. Progress asks that eligible attempts can eventually enter; bounded waiting strengthens this with a bound on overtaking. A design can be safe and still starve a thread forever. Locking only the write in a read-modify-write operation is insufficient because another thread may change the value after an unprotected read. Protect the whole invariant-preserving update. Condition variables normally wait by atomically releasing the associated lock and sleeping, then reacquire the lock before returning. Recheck the predicate in a loop because wakeups and competing consumers may leave it false. Semaphores store permits, whereas a condition notification does not generally store a future entitlement.')
R('7-deadlock','What a safety certificate guarantees',
'A safe state admits an order in which every process can receive its full remaining declared claim, complete and release resources. The safety algorithm does not predict actual scheduling; it constructs a witness that the system could satisfy even worst-case claims. Work is a temporary available-resource vector and must be compared componentwise with Need. Finishing a hypothetical process adds its held allocation to Work, producing a monotone increase. If no unfinished process is feasible, the current state lacks a safety certificate under those maximum claims. That is an unsafe-state finding, not proof that every process is already blocked. Deadlock prevention removes a necessary condition such as circular wait; avoidance uses future-claim information; detection evaluates actual outstanding requests and allocations.')
R('7-scheduling','Use events rather than averaging burst lengths',
'A scheduling decision can occur at an arrival, completion, quantum expiration or blocking event, depending on the policy. In nonpreemptive SJF, a newly arriving shorter job does not displace the running job; SRTF compares remaining time at relevant arrivals. Round robin appends unfinished work to a ready queue at quantum expiry, but arrivals and a simultaneous expiry need an explicitly stated tie convention. Waiting time is accumulated time in the ready queue. For a single CPU-only burst, turnaround equals waiting plus service, which permits subtraction; for workloads with I/O, blocked time must also be accounted for. Response time ends at the first CPU dispatch. Draw a separate timeline for each policy, and calculate per-process values before taking their arithmetic mean.')
R('7-virtual-files','Page-table walks and replacement state',
'Paging preserves the offset because a page and its physical frame have equal size. The page-table entry supplies the frame number and permission/residency metadata. A TLB caches recent translations, so a miss can be resolved by an ordinary page-table walk without disk access. A nonresident page is a page fault and invokes a much slower path that can include eviction, dirty-page writeback, load and restart. Replacement policies have distinct state: FIFO records load order, LRU records last reference, and optimal replacement uses the future reference sequence as a comparison benchmark. A hit changes LRU recency but not FIFO age. Compare page faults on the same initial frame state and reference string. Effective access time needs complete mutually exclusive paths; a miss penalty defined as extra time must not have the baseline counted twice.')
R('7-files','Data reachability, metadata and disk movement',
'An indirect block contains addresses, not user payload. If a block contains p addresses, one single-indirect pointer reaches p data blocks, a double-indirect pointer reaches up to p², and a triple-indirect pointer up to p³. Add direct blocks, then multiply reachable data blocks by data-block size. Metadata space and metadata I/O are separate counts: following a double-indirect pointer may read two index blocks before the data block unless cached. Linked allocation makes sequential access natural but locating an arbitrary logical block requires link traversal. Contiguous allocation permits arithmetic location but constrains growth and can suffer external fragmentation. Disk scheduling calculations apply to a moving-head model: add absolute differences of consecutive head positions and follow the specified direction/end/wrap rules.')
R('8-er-relations','Keys express constraints rather than sample coincidences',
'A candidate key is a minimal set of attributes that uniquely identifies every possible legal row under the schema constraints. Seeing distinct names in one sample table does not prove name is a key. A superkey may contain unnecessary attributes; a primary key is one chosen candidate key. A relationship can need its own attributes: a grade belongs to a student’s enrollment in a course, rather than to the student or course alone. For repeated enrollments, the student/course pair ceases to identify each event and a term or attempt identifier is needed. Weak entities depend on an owner plus their partial key. Foreign-key constraints enforce that referenced rows exist, but cardinality, nullability and total participation can need additional uniqueness or application/database constraints.')
R('8-queries','Trace bags, nulls and grouping explicitly',
'Under SQL bag semantics, a join can multiply duplicates: if a key has two matching left rows and three matching right rows, their equality join contributes six rows. DISTINCT removes duplicates at its specified stage and cannot be assumed throughout. WHERE evaluates predicates on source rows, then GROUP BY partitions survivors and HAVING filters completed groups. Null comparisons normally yield UNKNOWN, so NULL=NULL does not become TRUE; use IS NULL to test nullness. COUNT(*) counts rows, while COUNT(column) and AVG(column) ignore nulls. For an empty input without grouping, COUNT returns zero while many other aggregates return NULL. NOT IN can be poisoned by a null comparison; reasoning through the three-valued truth conditions is safer than blindly rewriting it as an anti-join.')
R('8-normalization','Closure, decomposition and independent guarantees',
'Attribute closure is an inference procedure: start with X, apply any FD whose entire left side is present, add its right side, and repeat to a fixed point. A key needs closure equal to all relation attributes and minimality under attribute removal. BCNF restricts every nontrivial determinant to a superkey; 3NF additionally permits a prime right-hand attribute. Lossless decomposition means every legal original relation is exactly reconstructed by joining its projections. It does not merely mean that tables share a column. Dependency preservation means constraints can be enforced within component relations without joining them. Projection includes all implied dependencies on each component, not just original FDs with visibly matching columns. A decomposition can satisfy one guarantee and fail the other, so state separate proofs.')
R('8-storage-index','Derive capacity from page layout',
'An internal B+ tree page with f children normally needs f pointers and f−1 separator keys; a leaf page instead needs key/record entries and a next-leaf pointer, potentially with other metadata. Subtract reserved bytes before deriving each capacity. Maximum fanout is not minimum occupancy: split rules and root exceptions affect minimum and maximum tree heights. Search chooses an internal pointer using separators until a leaf, then checks the actual key. A range query locates its first leaf once and follows leaf links, but fetching records through an unclustered index can add scattered data-page I/O. A clustered layout tends to group nearby key values in nearby data pages. Counting tree levels alone estimates index traversal, not necessarily the entire query’s disk cost.')
R('8-transactions','Three graphs of reasoning: conflict, reads and locks',
'Conflict ordering constrains transactions when actions from different transactions touch the same item and at least one is a write. Read/read order adds no conflict edge. A directed cycle prevents any serial order satisfying all conflict constraints. Recovery instead follows which write supplied each read: committing a reader before that writer risks a committed dependency on an aborted value. Cascadeless schedules prevent reading uncommitted writes; strict schedules also prevent overwriting uncommitted writes. Two-phase locking forbids new lock acquisitions after the first release and guarantees conflict serializability, but can deadlock. Strict 2PL holds exclusive locks until completion, preventing dirty reads/writes of protected items; terminology for holding all locks is often called rigorous 2PL. Distinguish protocol rules from properties inferred for one particular schedule.')
R('9-layers-switching','Serialization, propagation and pipeline overlap',
'A link serializes a packet by placing its bits onto the medium over L/R seconds. Once a bit is launched, propagation carries it across the link over distance/speed seconds. Doubling packet length doubles serialization but does not change an individual bit’s propagation delay. A store-and-forward router must receive the complete packet before starting its outgoing serialization. Across equal-rate links, successive packets can overlap on different links; the first packet pays the full path serialization, while later packets emerge at the bottleneck packet interval under the simplified no-queuing model. Routers replace link-layer framing for the next hop while forwarding toward the network-layer destination. Circuit setup and reserved capacity belong to a different service model than ordinary statistical multiplexing.')
R('9-link-layer','Why sequence-space bounds prevent ambiguity',
'A sender may have multiple frames outstanding while acknowledgments are delayed. Sequence numbers wrap, so a delayed old frame must not be mistaken for a new frame with the same number. Selective repeat accepts and buffers a window of out-of-order frames; disjoint old and new windows require the usual half-sequence-space bound. Go-back-N’s receiver ordinarily accepts only the next expected frame, yielding a different maximum sender window. State those receiver models before using memorized bounds. CRC treats bitstrings as polynomials over GF(2), so subtraction is XOR without borrow. Appending r zeros multiplies data by xʳ; the division remainder produces a codeword divisible by the generator. Detection guarantees depend on the error polynomial and generator, not just the remainder’s length.')
R('9-routing','Local advertisements versus global topology',
'A distance-vector router stores estimates learned from neighbors and chooses the neighbor minimizing link cost plus advertised remaining distance. If a neighbor’s route actually depends on the router itself, stale messages after a failure can create a loop. Split horizon and poison reverse suppress some immediate feedback, but are not universal proofs against every multi-router convergence problem. Link-state routers flood link information so each can compute from a topology snapshot; inconsistent or old snapshots can still produce transient behavior. After routes are installed, forwarding chooses among destination prefixes by longest match. A more specific route overrides a broader matching route even if the broader one appears first or has a lower cost metric; route selection within a prefix and forwarding specificity are separate stages.')
R('9-ipv4','Bits determine alignment and aggregation',
'A /p prefix fixes the first p address bits and leaves 32−p host bits. The network address sets those remaining bits to zero; under ordinary subnet semantics the directed broadcast sets them all to one. A /26 leaves six bits, so last-octet boundaries occur at multiples of 64. Aggregating two /p blocks into /p−1 is valid only if their prefixes differ in the last prefix bit and share all earlier bits. Numerically adjacent blocks can cross an alignment boundary and fail this test. Conventional host-count subtraction reserves network and broadcast addresses, but /31 point-to-point addressing and /32 host routes use different conventions. Private addressing, NAT and CIDR solve different needs: a private address is not itself a routing prefix-length rule.')
R('9-fragmentation','Offsets describe the original payload',
'Each fragment is a separate IP packet with its own header and total length. The offset identifies where its data belongs in the original IP payload; counting previous fragment headers would misplace reassembly. Nonfinal payload sizes must be multiples of eight because the next offset has eight-byte granularity. The last payload may have a remainder. Fragmentation adds header overhead, so summed transmitted fragment lengths exceed original length when more than one fragment is produced. Identification, source, destination and protocol help associate fragments; offset and MF locate the payload boundaries. If an already fragmented packet is fragmented again, offsets remain relative to the original payload, and a piece that is last only within its parent fragment can still require MF=1. IPv6 routers do not perform the same in-network fragmentation process as IPv4 routers.')
R('9-tcp-web','Track byte intervals and independent windows',
'A segment with sequence s and b data bytes covers the inclusive byte interval s through s+b−1. If all earlier bytes have arrived, the cumulative ACK is s+b, the next expected byte. Out-of-order arrival need not advance that ACK across a gap. SYN and FIN each consume sequence-number space even without application data. The receive window advertises buffer availability; congestion window is a sender-side network-control quantity. New sending allowance is the smaller window minus bytes already outstanding, bounded below by zero. A large window alone does not guarantee its window/RTT throughput bound is attained: bottleneck link rate, loss, application supply and startup can constrain it further. Retransmission, flow control and congestion control must be tracked as separate mechanisms.')
R('9-sockets','Message framing over a byte stream',
'TCP supplies reliable ordered bytes but no boundary markers between application writes. A sender can write a length header and payload separately, while a receiver gets a combined chunk, a partial header or a partial payload. A correct receive loop accumulates enough bytes for the agreed header, validates the declared length, then accumulates exactly that many payload bytes. Remaining bytes may belong to the next message and should not be discarded. A return of zero conventionally indicates orderly end of stream, which differs from a short positive read. A listening TCP socket accepts multiple connections into separate connected sockets. UDP preserves datagram boundaries under its API but does not add TCP’s ordered delivery/retransmission guarantees; an application must choose its own reliability semantics.')
R('9-dns','Delegation and caches change the query path',
'The root directs a resolver toward a top-level-domain authority, which can direct it toward an authoritative server for a narrower zone. These are referrals, not necessarily the final host address. A recursive resolver performs this work on behalf of its client; iterative exchanges supply answers or referrals one step at a time. Cached answers and delegation records have separate TTLs, so a cache can skip some exchanges but still need an authoritative lookup. A CNAME is an alias whose target may require further resolution; MX selects mail-exchanger names, not automatically their numeric IP addresses. Delay counting should identify which requests are serial and which cached records exist at the start. UDP is common for queries, but TCP and other DNS transports also exist; protocol correctness is not equivalent to a single UDP exchange.')
R('9-http','Build an explicit latency model',
'For one object over a fresh TCP connection, a simplified model counts one RTT for establishment and one RTT for the request and start of response, then object transmission. It omits DNS, TLS, congestion startup and queuing unless supplied. Persistence reuses the connection and avoids repeating setup. HTTP/1.1 without pipelining can still issue requests sequentially; HTTP/2 multiplexes streams over a connection; HTTP/3 uses QUIC streams over UDP. Cache freshness can avoid a network request, while conditional validation still contacts the server and may receive 304 rather than a complete body. An HTTP error status is a valid application response; connection refusal occurs before such a response. Any numerical RTT answer must specify connection reuse, parallel connections and which transfers overlap.')
